@* Badge's Overview, written by hand after Tag's: status and accent colors, variants, dots, counts
   and icons, badges inside headings and buttons, and the badges other components draw themselves.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A badge is a small label next to something. It suits a status ("Paid"), a category ("Beta") or a count. It's text, not a control.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For a keyword that's a link or can be removed, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/tag">Tag</Anchor>. For a message about something, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/alert">Alert</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Status</Heading>
<TextBlock>The status is what the badge means, and it sets the badge's color: Neutral (the default), Info, Success, Warning or Danger. Set <Code>Status</Code> to choose one. For a color of your own, set <Code>Accent</Code> instead, such as <Code>Accent.Primary</Code> for a brand-colored label or <Code>Accent.Of("#0d9488")</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="badge-statuses" />

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>The variant sets how strongly the badge stands out: <Code>Soft</Code> (the default), <Code>Solid</Code>, <Code>Outline</Code> or <Code>Plain</Code>. Set <Code>Variant</Code> to choose one. <Code>Plain</Code> is the text alone, in the color's text shade; it suits quiet counts, such as the totals beside sidebar links.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="badge-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">Dots, counts and icons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A badge can show a colored dot or an icon before its text, and a count can have fully rounded ends. To show a dot before a status, set <Code>Dot</Code>; to round a count's ends, set <Code>Pill</Code>. To show an icon before the text, set <Code>StartIcon</Code>; with <Code>Dot</Code> set too, only the dot shows.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="badge-dots-counts" />
<TextBlock>For a compact badge for counts, set <Code>Size="BadgeSize.Sm"</Code>. The color, the dot and the icon don't reach screen readers, so the text has to say what it means: "Overdue", not a red badge reading "3 days".</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">In headings and buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A badge sits in a line of text, so you can put it inside a heading or a button's label as it is. In a button, a small solid pill (<Code>Size="BadgeSize.Sm"</Code>) looks best beside the label.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="badge-inline" />
<TextBlock>Screen readers read a count as just the number, as part of the button's name ("Inbox 4"). Put it where the words next to it say what it counts.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Some components show their own badges. On a <Anchor Href="docs/components/nav-menu">NavItem</Anchor>, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/tabs">Tab</Anchor> or a <Anchor Href="docs/components/tab-nav">TabLink</Anchor>, set <Code>Badge</Code> rather than nesting a Badge. A NavItem also has <Code>BadgeStatus</Code>, <Code>BadgeAccent</Code> and <Code>BadgeVariant</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Badge Status="Status.Success">Paid</Badge>
        <Badge Status="Status.Warning" Dot>In review</Badge>
        <Badge Accent="Accent.Primary" Variant="BadgeVariant.Solid" Pill>12</Badge>
        """;
}
